<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.1.4: Creating Expressions to Estimate Cost, Part 2</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00058</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.1.4: Creating Expressions to Estimate Cost, Part 2</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cb92495e-810a-4f69-af9f-ea431b0df951</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">At the end of the school year, all students in the ninth grade are invited to an ice cream party. Use the skills you learned from planning a pizza party to answer the following:</para>
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Identify and describe at least one letter that you will use to represent the cost of something you need to buy for the party.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00004">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> = number of different toppings one student selects</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> = number of different scoops of ice cream one student requests</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> = total number of ninth grade students at party</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> = cost of paper products like napkins, bowls, and spoons</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Write an expression that could represent an estimated cost for the party using that letter. Note, you may use other letters, too, if needed.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
          <list id="list-00002">
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.75</m:mn><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1.5</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> total cost</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4.00</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> total cost</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.50</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>0.08</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.50</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> total cost</item>
            <item><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>125</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> total cost</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Describe your expression in words.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item>Each scoop of ice cream costs $0.75 and the cost of each topping is $1.50 which sum to the cost of one bowl of ice cream. Now we multiply the cost of one bowl of ice cream by the number of students in the class.</item>
            <item>A flat cost of $4 per bowl of ice cream for each student plus a charge of $15 for the bowls, spoons, and napkins.</item>
            <item>There are <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> students in the ninth grade. A packaged ice cream cone is about $1.50, and tax is about 8%.</item>
            <item>There are 125 students in the ninth grade. Suppose a tub of ice cream costs $5 and is enough for 6 students. A pack of 24 cones costs about $3, and 6 packs are needed. A pack of 150 paper napkins costs <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> dollars.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Using the letter you identified in question 1, describe the values that would be reasonable for the quantity that the letter represents.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00013">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item>You may choose to limit each student to selecting at most five toppings, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, per bowl of ice cream. Or, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>The number of scoops of ice cream, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, provided to each student should not exceed three. Or, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>s</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
            <item>The number of students, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, at the ninth grade party will probably be less than 730. Or,<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>730</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>A whole number between 90 and 100 is a reasonable value for the number of students in ninth grade, or 90 <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
            <item>A number less than $4 but more than $1 is reasonable for the cost of a pack of paper napkins, or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> 4.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
